1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,000
Welcome back.

2
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,000
When you move an app to a newer cloud model,

3
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:05,000
you can hit a surprise.

4
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:07,000
A call that worked on the old model

5
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:09,000
suddenly returns an error,

6
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:11,000
and nothing in your logic changed.

7
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:13,000
This lesson covers why that happens,

8
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:15,000
which parameters usually break,

9
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:20,000
and why thinking has to be tested on the target model. Let's dive in.

10
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Let's start with three terms that frame everything else.

11
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:26,000
First, a breaking change.

12
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,000
That's when a newer model generation rejects a parameter,

13
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,000
an older generation happily accepted,

14
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:35,000
so a request that once worked now fails.

15
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:38,000
Second, the 400 error.

16
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:39,000
When you send a parameter

17
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:41,000
the target model no longer supports,

18
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:43,000
the API answers with a 400.

19
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:46,000
It means bad request, your input,

20
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:49,000
not a server outage on Anthropic's side.

21
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,000
Third, model-specific behavior.

22
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Which parameters a call accepts

23
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:57,000
depends on the exact model you're hitting,

24
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,000
so identical code can pass on one model

25
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,000
and fail on another.

26
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Breaking change, 400 error, model-specific behavior.

27
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:07,000
Keep those three in mind,

28
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:09,000
because the rest of this lesson

29
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:11,000
is really about spotting and avoiding them.

30
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:16,000
So why would a call that's worked for months suddenly break?

31
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:19,000
It comes down to how model generations evolve.

32
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:23,000
Anthropic doesn't freeze the API surface forever.

33
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:27,000
As newer models ship, some older parameters get removed,

34
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:30,000
because a better mechanism replaced them.

35
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:31,000
On current models,

36
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:34,000
sampling controls like temperature are gone.

37
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:36,000
Final turn pre-fill is gone.

38
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:40,000
The old budget-based thinking setting is gone.

39
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:42,000
None of those were deleted to annoy you.

40
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:46,000
Each was retired in favor of a cleaner approach.

41
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:48,000
But here's the catch for migration.

42
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:50,000
When you point old code at a new model,

43
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:53,000
the request body still carries those retired parameters,

44
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:56,000
and the model rejects them with a 400.

45
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:59,000
The code didn't change.

46
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:00,000
The rules underneath it did.

47
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:05,000
Let me make this concrete with a before and after.

48
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:09,000
Before migration, your app runs on an older model.

49
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:11,000
The request sets a temperature,

50
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:13,000
maybe pre-fills the start of the assistance reply

51
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:15,000
to force a format,

52
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:18,000
and it comes back with a clean response every time.

53
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:19,000
Everything's fine.

54
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:20,000
Then you migrate.

55
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:23,000
You swap in a current model, like Opus 5,

56
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:26,000
and you change nothing else, because why would you?

57
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:29,000
If you do the exact same request, body returns a 400.

58
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:31,000
The temperature and the pre-fill

59
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:34,000
that were perfectly valid a moment ago

60
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:36,000
are rejected outright.

61
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,000
Same code, same prompt, different model, different rules.

62
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:43,000
That gap between what the old model tolerated

63
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:45,000
and what the new one accepts

64
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:47,000
is exactly where migration bugs live.

65
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:51,000
Let's name the three parameters that break most often,

66
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:54,000
because these are the usual suspects.

67
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:56,000
The first is sampling parameters.

68
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Temperature, top P, and top K return a 400

69
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:01,000
on the current tier.

70
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:06,000
Fable 5, Opus 5, Opus 4.8, and Sonnet 5.

71
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:10,000
You steer behavior with prompting and effort now instead.

72
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:13,000
The second is final turn pre-fill.

73
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:16,000
Putting words in the assistance mouth on the last turn

74
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:20,000
to shape the output is rejected on those same four models.

75
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:23,000
The third is the legacy thinking budget.

76
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:25,000
Setting thinking to enabled with a token budget

77
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:30,000
returns a 400 on the same four models as well.

78
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:33,000
Sampling, pre-fill, thinking budget.

79
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:35,000
If your old code touches any of these,

80
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:37,000
expect a 400 on a current model.

81
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:40,000
Let's slow down on sampling,

82
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:43,000
because developers reach for it out of habit.

83
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:45,000
On older models, you'd tune temperature

84
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:48,000
to make output more or less random,

85
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:51,000
and maybe top P or top K alongside it.

86
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:56,000
On current models like Fable 5, Opus 5, Opus 4.8,

87
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:59,000
and Sonnet 5, those parameters are removed,

88
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:02,000
and sending any of them returns a 400.

89
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:04,000
So what do you do instead?

90
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:08,000
You steer with prompting and with the effort level.

91
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:10,000
There's a related myth worth killing too.

92
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:12,000
People set temperature to zero,

93
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:16,000
expecting perfectly identical output every time.

94
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:19,000
That was never truly guaranteed, even on the old models.

95
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:23,000
So don't cling to sampling knobs for determinism.

96
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:25,000
They're gone on current models,

97
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:27,000
and they never delivered that guarantee anyway.

98
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:29,000
Here's the good news.

99
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:32,000
For each retired parameter, there's a clear replacement.

100
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:35,000
So migration is mostly a set of swaps.

101
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:37,000
Three of them cover most cases.

102
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:40,000
First, sampling to effort.

103
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:42,000
Instead of tuning temperature, you set the effort level

104
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:45,000
and lean on your prompt to shape behavior.

105
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:48,000
Second, budget to adaptive.

106
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:51,000
Instead of the legacy budget tokens thinking mode,

107
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:54,000
current models use adaptive thinking,

108
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:56,000
where the model decides how much to think,

109
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:59,000
and there's no budget to hand-tune.

110
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:01,000
Third, pre-fill to structured.

111
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:03,000
Instead of pre-filling the assistant turn,

112
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:06,000
you ask for a structured output format,

113
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:09,000
or you put the instruction in the system prompt.

114
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:12,000
Sampling to effort, budget to adaptive,

115
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:13,000
pre-fill to structured.

116
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:15,000
Learn those three swaps,

117
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:17,000
and most migrations get straightforward.

118
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:21,000
Now the part that surprises people most.

119
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:24,000
Thinking behavior isn't uniform across the lineup,

120
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:26,000
so you can't assume it ports forward.

121
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:31,000
On Opus 5 and Sonnet 5, adaptive thinking is the path.

122
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:36,000
And if you send the old enabled with a budget style, you get a 400.

123
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:39,000
On Haiku 4.5, it's the opposite.

124
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:41,000
Haiku still uses the budget tokens mode

125
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:44,000
and has no adaptive thinking at all.

126
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:46,000
And Fable 5 is different again.

127
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:48,000
Thinking is always on there,

128
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:51,000
and trying to disable it is rejected.

129
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:54,000
So the same thinking code can be correct on one model

130
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:56,000
and broken on the next.

131
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:00,000
That's why you can't port thinking configuration blindly.

132
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:03,000
You check what the specific target model supports,

133
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:04,000
then match your call to it.

134
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:07,000
So what's the takeaway you act on?

135
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:10,000
Never assume parameters carry forward.

136
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:12,000
The safe habit is simple.

137
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:14,000
Before you trust migrated code,

138
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:16,000
run it against the exact model

139
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:17,000
you plan to use in production,

140
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:20,000
not a similar one, the actual one.

141
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:22,000
When a call fails right after a migration

142
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:24,000
and returns a 400,

143
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:27,000
don't start debugging your own logic first.

144
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:29,000
That status code is pointing at a removed

145
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:31,000
or unsupported parameter

146
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:34,000
far more often than a bug in your handling.

147
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:36,000
And read the migration notes for the target model

148
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:37,000
before you cut over,

149
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:40,000
so you already know which parameters changed.

150
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:42,000
Test against the target.

151
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:44,000
Read the 400 as a parameter signal

152
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:47,000
and check the migration guide first.

153
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:52,000
That's how you catch these before your users do. Let's recap.

154
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:54,000
Newer models can reject parameters

155
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:56,000
that older models accepted.

156
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:59,000
So a once valid call can return a 400 error.

157
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:03,000
Just remember, sampling params, final turn pre-fill,

158
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:06,000
and legacy budget tokens, thinking, break first.

159
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:09,000
And thinking stays model specific.

160
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:13,000
That covers when a newer model rejects old params.

161
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:15,000
When you're ready, let's jump into the next one.

